1. Introduction
Noble gases and binary mixtures are being considered as working fluids for space power systems and terrestrial electric power plants employing Closed Brayton Cycle (CBC) and gas turbo-machines. CBC engines are an attractive energy conversion option for use with a space nuclear reactor heat source to generate hundreds of kilowatts of electrical power for future planetary exploration missions and human outposts on the moon and Mars for many years. The typical operating pressure in these systems is 2.0
MPa and the reactor exit and compressor exit temperatures are typically 1200–1400 K and 400 K, respectively. Terrestrial nuclear power plants for hydrogen production and co-generation of electricity at high efficiency >45% typically operate at higher pressure of 7.0 MPa and temperatures from 400 to 1300 K. For these terrestrial power plants, pure helium is the preferred working fluid, while space nuclear reactor power systems with CBC employ a binary mixture of He–Xe having a molecular weight of 40 g/mole. Helium (He) has the best thermal properties of all pure noble gases, but when mixed with heavier noble gases, the thermal properties of the resulting binary mixtures could be superior to those of the pure gases. Recently, El-Genk and Tournier [1] have shown that binary mixtures
