reduced density, q/qcr = 0.2 and 300 K, the thermal conductivity predictions for Ne, Ar, Kr and Xe using the Chapman–Enskog approach are lower than the measured values by 7%, 12%, 16% and 22%, respectively (Fig. 1). For He at the same operating condition, however, the difference between the predicted and measured values is much smaller, 2%. The difference between predictions and measured values of the thermal conductivity of dense and dilute gases increases with increasing pressure and/or molecular weight; for pure helium, it is 1.2% at 5 MPa and 4.8% at 20 MPa. For the heaviest noble gas of Xe at the same pressures of 5 and 20 MPa, the difference between predicted and measured values of the thermal conductivity is as much as 40% and 90% (Fig. 1). Although the dynamic viscosities of the dilute gases are directly related to their thermal conductivities, the differences between the predicted and measured values of viscosity are smaller than for the thermal conductivity. At 5.0 MPa and 770 K, the difference between measured and calculated values of the thermal conductivity of Xe using the Chapman–Enskog approach is 5%, but < 5% at temperatures > 125 K, 200 K, 440 K and 570 K for He, Ne, Ar and Kr, respectively. At lower temperatures and pressures higher than 5.0 MPa, the difference between the calculated thermal conductivities of dilute gases and the measured values exceeds 5%. Because of its very low critical temperature (5.2 K), the difference between measured and calculated thermal conductivities of He (as well as Ne, Tcr = 44.6 K) at typical operating conditions in terrestrial, gas cooled reactors (300–1300 K and 1–20 MPa), is very small. At 2.0 MPa, the difference between measured and calculated thermal conductivity of Xe at a typical compressor temperature of 400 K is 8%. In order to develop accurate correlations of the thermophysical properties of the dense noble gases and mixtures as functions of molar composition, temperature, and pressure, an extensive experi