3. Properties database
The literature is reviewed for measured data of the transport and thermodynamic properties of the pure noble gases, He, Ne, Ar, Kr and Xe and their 10 binary mixtures as functions of temperature and pressure. The molecular weight and critical parameters of the pure noble gases are readily available and accurate; the values recommended by the National Institute of Standards and Technology [10] and listed in Table 1 are used in this work. The compiled database for the dynamic viscosity and thermal conductivity are summarized in Tables 2 and 3, respectively. For clarity, the experimental data are organized into four groups: (a) data pertaining to the pure noble gases at dilute density (i.e. pressures 6 0.1 MPa); (b) data pertaining to the binary mixtures of dilute noble gases; (c) high-pressure (high-density) data pertaining to the pure noble gases (0.1 < P < 51 MPa); and (d) high-pressure data pertaining to the binary mixtures of noble gases. For each binary gas mixture, Tables 2 and 3 list the source references, the number of data points reported and considered in this work, and the temperature, pressure and the range of the reduced density, when relevant. The last columns of these tables give the maximum deviation between the reported
data and the predictions using the semi-empirical correlations developed in this work. As these tables indicate, the number of data points collected is large and the temperature range is usually extensive, covering the full range between low values in the critical region to values as high as 1200 K and beyond. As will be shown in the following sections and listed in Tables 2–4, the developed semi-empirical relations in this work for the thermophysical properties of noble gases and binary mixtures accurately predict the great majority of the compiled experimental data. The largest deviations between the predictions and the data in Tables 2–4 are generally caused by the discrepancies in data reported by the different investigators, or for data measured near the critical point, which is not of interest in this work. The densities of the pure noble gases and their mixtures are calculated using the virial equation of state, which requires knowledge of the second (B) and third (C) virial coefficients of the pure gases, and the interaction, second virial coefficients (B12) of the binary mixtures, which are all functions of temperature. The compiled database for these virial coefficients is summarized in Table 4. While much data is available for the pure noble gases, only a few data could be found for the interaction virial coefficients B12. The maximum error in the second virial coefficients is reported in Table 4 as an actual deviation (in cm3/mole), since these coefficients span a wide range of negative and positive values. Many of the investigators who measured the thermal conductivity of dense pure gases and dense binary mixtures
