4. The law of corresponding states for dense gases
The effects of high-density and/or high-pressure on the thermophysical properties of noble gases are correlated using the two-parameter law of corresponding states [88]. The two representative parameters used are the critical temperature Tcr and critical density qcr, instead of the potential parameters e and r in the Chapman–Enskog approach for dilute gases. The critical parameters of the pure gases are readily available and accurate [10], and thus the present model can easily be applied and extended to multi-component mixtures in the moderate to the highdensity region. The principle of corresponding states is a straightforward dimensional analysis that unfortunately generated little interest in the past due to the lack of experimental data. At a time when little was known about intermolecular forces, the law of corresponding states had been associated with quantitatively inaccurate equations of states, especially the van der Waal’s state equation [3]. Much later, the lack of knowledge of the transport properties led investigators to associate the law with approximate models of the intermolecular potential, i.e. the Lennard-Jones (12-6) model [88]. These unfortunate associations prevented many from realizing the potential of the very powerful law of corresponding states. The present work of using the law of
