6.5. Dynamic viscosity
Fig. 33a and b show that the dynamic viscosity of the noble gases and their binary mixtures almost doubles as the temperature increases from 400 K to 1200 K. The dynamic viscosities of the binary gas mixtures of He with either Ne or Ar increase from the lower value of pure He to those of Ne and Ar, as the molecular weights of these mixtures increase. Pure Ne has the highest dynamic viscosity, followed by Kr, Xe, Ar and finally He. Thus, when mixing He with Ne, or any of the higher molecular weight pure noble gases of Ar, Kr or Xe, the dynamic viscosity of the resulting binary gas mixture decreases as the molecular weight decreases to that of He, or increases to that of the heavier noble gas in the mixture as the molecular weight increases (Fig. 33a and b). Fig. 34 shows the errors in the dynamic viscosity of pure Ar and Xe, and He–Xe gas mixture with a molecular weight of 40 g/mole, due to neglecting the effect of pressure. The values for pure xenon are included in this figure for comparison. The properties of this gas exhibit the largest effect of pressure, and Xe is a component of the favored He–Xe binary mixture for space nuclear power systems with CBC engines. A close examination of Fig. 34 with Fig. 32 reveals that the errors in the dynamic viscosities of the noble gases and binary mixtures due to neglecting the effect of pressure are smaller than those of the thermal conductivities. One reason is the peculiar behavior of He, whose dynamic viscosity is independent of pressure [35,36,38]. For example, at 300 K and 2.0 MPa, the error of neglecting the pressure effect on the viscosity of the binary mixture of He72–Xe28 is 0.5% (Fig. 34), compared to 0.8% in the thermal conductivity of the same mixture (Fig. 32). For Ar at the same conditions, the error of neglecting the effect of pressure on the thermal conductivity and dynamic viscosity is higher, 4.2% and 1.2%, respectively. At 2.0 MPa and 300 K, the errors in the thermal conductivity and viscosity of Xe due to neglecting the effect of pressure are 12.3% and 4.3% (Figs. 32 and 34). At compressor inlet temperature of 300 K and pressure of 5 MPa, neglecting the effect of pressure on the thermal conductivity and the dynamic viscosity of Ar results in errors of 11.1% and 4.0%, respectively, compared to only 4.2% and 1.2% at 2.0 MPa (Figs. 32 and 34).
6.6. Prandtl number
