7. Summary and conclusions
An extensive experimental database of thermodynamic and transport properties of noble gases and their binary mixtures, at temperatures up to 1400 K and pressures up to 20 MPa, is compiled, and used to quantify the effect of pressure. Accurate semi-empirical relationships of these properties are developed based on the kinetic theory approach for dilute gases, and using the two-parameter principle of corresponding states to account for the effect of pressure. The developed expressions for the gas properties are valid at temperatures between the critical value, Tcr and 1400 K, pressures up to 20 MPa, and densities up to the critical values, qcr. Although no attempts are made in this work to predict the gas properties in the critical region, where both temperature and pressure approach their critical values simultaneously, the present semi-empirical relations predict the gas properties in the critical region with a reasonable accuracy for T > Tcr, but cannot be used in the liquid–vapor phase change region.
