The comparison of the developed relations for the thermodynamic and transport properties of the pure noble gases and binary mixtures with the compiled measurements database quantified the errors in the properties, due to neglecting the effect of pressure. Results show that at typical operating conditions encountered in space nuclear power systems with CBC engines (T P 400 K and P 6 2.0 MPa), the pure gases of He and Ne, and the binary gas mixtures with a molecular weight M < 40 g/mole behave like perfect gases, and the errors of neglecting the effects of pressure on their compressibility factor, specific heats and transport properties are <1%. The effect of pressure on the thermodynamic and transport properties increases with increasing the molecular weight of the noble gas and/or of the binary mixture. However, the effect of pressure on the properties of the pure gases is more pronounced than on the properties of the binary gas mixtures of identical molecular weight. For example, at 400 K and 2.0 MPa, the molar heat capacity, specific heat ratio and thermal conductivity of pure argon deviate from their ideal, diluted gas values by 2.3%, 1.8% and 4%, respectively, compared to 61% for the He72Xe28 binary gas mixture of the same molecular weight (M = 40 g/mole). When operating at higher densities or pressures >2.0 MPa, particularly for noble gases and binary mixtures with molecular weights >40 g/mole, the errors in the properties could be larger. In this case, the semi-empirical property relations developed in this work should be used in place of the Chapman–Enskog kinetic theory for dilute gases, to accurately account for the effects of temperature and pressure on the thermodynamic and transport properties.
